    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basarab-i">Basarab I</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-posada">Battle of Posada</a> in 1330 consolidated Wallachia as an independent principality.</li><li>Wallachia accepted Ottoman suzerainty in 1417 and kept it until the mid-nineteenth century, yet preserved its own princes, boyars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> throughout.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, attested from 1385, was part of Wallachian society until the law of February 1856 freed all slaves as taxpayers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>, angered when Vlad III strengthened Wallachia's borders and disrupted their trade routes, spread grotesque poems painting him as a drinker of blood; those tales inspired the main character of Bram Stoker's Dracula in 1897.</p></aside>
    </section>

